CAT = "ieqfaRedress"; SEG = "RED"
Q = [
("A consumer is unhappy with how her insurer handled a claim. Before the Financial Services and Pensions Ombudsman will investigate, she must usually:",
 "Use the insurer's complaints process", ["Issue High Court proceedings against it first", "Get the Central Bank to agree", "Pay a fee to open a complaint"],
 "The FSPO expects the provider to have a chance to resolve the complaint through its own process first. The FSPO's service is free."),
("The Financial Services and Pensions Ombudsman was formed by merging:",
 "The FSO and the Pensions Ombudsman", ["The CCPC and the Pensions Authority", "The Central Bank and the Revenue", "Two credit union supervisory bodies in 2018"],
 "The FSPO was established on 1 January 2018 under the Financial Services and Pensions Ombudsman Act 2017, combining the Financial Services Ombudsman and the Pensions Ombudsman."),
("In general, a complaint must reach the FSPO within how long of the conduct complained of?",
 "6 years", ["2 years", "12 years", "No limit applies"],
 "The general time limit is 6 years from the conduct. Long-term financial services can also be complained about within 3 years of becoming aware of the conduct, subject to conditions."),
("A consumer only recently discovered a problem with an endowment policy sold ten years ago. For long-term financial services, the FSPO can also accept a complaint made within:",
 "3 years of becoming aware", ["6 months of becoming aware", "1 year of the policy ending", "10 years of the sale date"],
 "For long-term financial services there is an alternative limit of 3 years from the date the consumer became aware, or ought reasonably to have become aware, of the conduct, subject to the FSPO's conditions."),
("A consumer or provider unhappy with a legally binding FSPO decision can appeal to:",
 "The High Court", ["The Central Bank", "The District Court", "The Department of Finance"],
 "FSPO decisions are legally binding, subject only to an appeal to the High Court."),
("Which of these can the FSPO direct a provider to do after upholding a complaint?",
 "Pay the complainant compensation", ["Pay a fine to the Exchequer", "Suspend its directors from office for a year", "Withdraw its Central Bank licence"],
 "The FSPO can direct a provider to rectify conduct and pay compensation. Fines and licence decisions belong to the Central Bank's enforcement powers."),
("A bank authorised in Ireland fails. Its customers' eligible deposits are protected by:",
 "The Deposit Guarantee Scheme", ["The Insurance Compensation Fund", "The Investor Compensation Scheme", "The Social Insurance Fund"],
 "The Deposit Guarantee Scheme, run by the Central Bank, repays eligible deposits up to the EU harmonised limit if a bank, building society or credit union fails."),
("The Investor Compensation Scheme pays out when:",
 "A failed firm cannot return client assets", ["A share falls sharply in value", "A fund underperforms its benchmark", "An adviser recommends a poorly performing product"],
 "The scheme, run by the Investor Compensation Company DAC, covers clients of a failed investment firm or intermediary that cannot return their money or investments. It does not cover market losses."),
("A non-life insurer authorised in Ireland goes into liquidation. Policyholders' claims may be met from:",
 "The Insurance Compensation Fund", ["The Deposit Guarantee Scheme", "The Investor Compensation Scheme", "The Pensions Authority's levy fund"],
 "The Insurance Compensation Fund can pay certain claims against a non-life insurer in liquidation. It is a fund of last resort, separate from the Deposit Guarantee Scheme and the Investor Compensation Scheme."),
("Under the Code, a firm's written communications about its unregulated activities must warn consumers that the service:",
 "Has no statutory compensation cover", ["Is guaranteed by the Central Bank of Ireland", "Is taxed at a higher rate than other services", "Has been approved by the FSPO"],
 "The prescribed warning says the service does not require Central Bank authorisation and is not covered by Central Bank consumer protection rules or a statutory compensation scheme."),
("Under the GDPR, an organisation that suffers a personal data breach must normally notify the Data Protection Commission within:",
 "72 hours", ["24 hours", "7 days", "30 days"],
 "Article 33 requires notification without undue delay and, where feasible, within 72 hours of becoming aware, unless the breach is unlikely to result in a risk to people."),
("A client asks for a copy of all the personal data a broker holds about him. Under the GDPR, the broker must normally respond within:",
 "One month", ["Five working days", "Three months", "Six months"],
 "A subject access request must be answered without undue delay and within one month, extendable by two further months for complex or numerous requests."),
("Which GDPR principle is breached by collecting far more personal data than a purpose needs?",
 "Data minimisation", ["Storage limitation", "Integrity and confidentiality", "Accountability"],
 "Data must be adequate, relevant and limited to what is necessary for the purpose for which it is processed."),
("Before processing a client's health data for a life cover application, a firm generally needs:",
 "The client's explicit consent", ["The client's employer's approval", "A court order allowing it", "Nothing more than a phone call"],
 "Health data is special category data under the GDPR, so processing needs a further legal condition. For insurance applications this is usually the client's explicit consent."),
("Which body supervises compliance with data protection law in Ireland?",
 "The Data Protection Commission", ["The Central Bank of Ireland", "The Competition and Consumer Protection Commission", "The Financial Services and Pensions Ombudsman"],
 "The Data Protection Commission is Ireland's supervisory authority under the GDPR and the Data Protection Act 2018."),
("Which of these is a protected ground under the Equal Status Acts?",
 "Membership of the Traveller community", ["Owning a home in a particular county or town", "Having a low credit score", "Holding a full driving licence"],
 "The grounds include gender, civil status, family status, sexual orientation, religion, age, disability, race and membership of the Traveller community. Insurers may differentiate on some grounds where it is based on actuarial or statistical data."),
("Under the Code, the complaints procedure a firm uses must be:",
 "Shown prominently on its website", ["Sent only on request", "Kept confidential within the firm", "Approved yearly by the ombudsman"],
 "The firm must make its procedure available in a prominent place on all its websites and give a hard copy on request within 5 working days."),
("A firm must allow consumers to submit complaints:",
 "By post and electronically", ["Only through its website", "Only in person at one of its branches", "Only through a solicitor"],
 "The Code requires firms to permit and facilitate complaints in writing by post and by electronic means, and to offer the complaints process to anyone complaining orally."),
("When an insurer declines a claim, its decision letter must tell the claimant about:",
 "Any appeals mechanism it offers", ["The adviser's commission on the policy", "The insurer's share price that day", "Its claims staff's names"],
 "Within 5 working days of deciding a claim, the insurer must tell the claimant the outcome, any settlement offer, the reasons for any decline and any appeals mechanism it provides (Consumer Protection Regulations 2025, Regulation 364)."),
("Which body can a consumer contact about a regulated firm's conduct, though it does not resolve individual complaints?",
 "The Central Bank of Ireland", ["The Financial Services and Pensions Ombudsman", "The firm's own complaints unit", "The Small Claims Court"],
 "The Central Bank uses consumer information to supervise firms but does not adjudicate individual disputes; that is the FSPO's role."),
]
